{
  "abstract": "We study symmetric repairs of the known fractional-nine biplanar source $H=K_5\\vee C_8(2,3)$. A paired graph consists of two identical source sheets, independent fiber mates, and cross edges invariant under sheet exchange; the planar layers need not preserve that symmetry. We prove that every proper spanning subgraph of $H$ permits nine-color gluing with at most twenty-two literal cross edges. The bound is sharp for abstract paired graphs, with a nonbiplanar control at twenty-four edges. Combining palette certificates with one local Euler inequality, we then show that no biplanar member retaining at least sixty of the original sixty-six edges per sheet can require ten colors, irrespective of its flip history. The argument extends beyond source-edge deletions: all 715 ways to attach an extra vertex to three or four vertices of the relevant twelve-vertex cores are also closed at the same global edge budget. Hand matching reductions, a positive coloring bank, and compact RUP proofs establish the finite premises; an independent standard-library verifier checks complete coverage and every proof addition. Finally, exact certificates for the published eleven-flip transport explain its failure: fractional value drops at the first join-edge deletion, while ordinary chromatic number stays nine until a later heavy-heavy deletion. These are restricted repair obstructions, not a resolution of the Earth–Moon problem.",
